---
// design/09_FAQ.md, ui_ux/09_FAQ_UI.md. Every panel renders open by default (Accordion,
// task 1.10) — Block 4's accordion.ts collapses them once JS confirms it can run. Category
// "tabs" are real anchors jumping to each group, matching the site's established pattern
// (Solutions, Sectors) rather than JS-driven filtering.
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import Accordion from '../components/Accordion.astro';

const categories = [
	{ id: 'the-solution', label: 'The Solution' },
	{ id: 'the-research', label: 'The Research' },
	{ id: 'licensing', label: 'Licensing' },
	{ id: 'investing', label: 'Investing' },
	{ id: 'the-company', label: 'The Company' },
];
---

<Page title="FAQ">
	<section class="section container">
		<Eyebrow>FAQ</Eyebrow>
		<h1>Everything people ask. Answered completely.</h1>
		<nav class="faq-tabs" aria-label="Jump to a category">
			<a href="#the-solution">All</a>
			{categories.map((c) => <a href={`#${c.id}`}>{c.label}</a>)}
		</nav>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal" id="the-solution">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">THE SOLUTION</Eyebrow>
		<div class="faq-group">
			<Accordion id="q-charter-partnership" question="What is a Charter Partnership?">
				<p>
					The Charter Partnership Programme is the pre-booking window for ORMAS solutions
					that are in build or in active development. Charter Partners negotiate terms
					before pricing is published. Once a solution reaches general availability,
					pricing is fixed. Charter Partners lock in flat-rate enterprise-wide pricing
					before pricing is standardised at general availability. Three slots are
					available per quarter.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-charter-get" question="What does a Charter Partner actually get?">
				<p>
					Five things. Your compliance workflow and edge cases become the baseline
					architecture of the solution — the product is built around your requirements,
					not a generic spec. Flat-rate enterprise-wide pricing locked for five years,
					before per-model consumption pricing is published at launch. Direct engineering
					access — the person who built ORMAS is your integration contact, not an account
					manager. A guaranteed deployment slot before the regulatory compliance window
					closes. And the first-mover position: institutions that define the compliance
					standard before it is imposed do not just comply — they set the benchmark
					others will follow.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-prebooking-close" question="When does the pre-booking window close?">
				<p>
					When the solution reaches general availability. At that point, pricing is
					published and fixed. Individual negotiation is not available post-launch. The
					timeline is tied to engineering milestones, not an arbitrary date. Four of the
					six pre-bookable solutions depend on a single validation study currently being
					scoped. The build timeline for the other two (Label Audit, Model Change Record)
					is weeks to months. Pre-booking now means locking terms before these milestones
					are met.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-prebooking-vs-launch"
				question="What is the difference between pre-booking pricing and launch pricing?"
			>
				<p>
					Pre-booking pricing is negotiated individually between us and the Charter
					Partner, taking into account the specific integration, data environment,
					regulatory framework, and deployment scope. Launch pricing will be a published
					rate card: annual institutional licence, per-solution, non-negotiable. The
					structure will shift toward per-model consumption pricing at GA. Charter
					Partners are not on the per-model model — they are on a flat institutional rate.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-multiple-prebook" question="Can I pre-book more than one solution?">
				<p>
					Yes. An enterprise substrate Charter Partnership covers the full portfolio
					under a single institutional licence ($500k–$1.5M). That conversation happens
					at /contact and is the highest-priority intake path. If you are deploying ORMAS
					across a full regulated or third-party-data model portfolio, the substrate tier
					is the right starting point.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-warning-light-only"
				question="Why is The Warning Light the only solution available to book today?"
			>
				<p>
					The Warning Light is the only solution where the core capability —
					per-component health monitoring during training — already runs in the existing
					codebase. The other solutions require additional engineering (Label Audit,
					Model Change Record) or a pending validation study (Source Separator, Certified
					Deletion, The Federated Node, The Update Engine). We publish the engineering
					status clearly because the alternative — selling something not yet built as
					available — is how a company loses a regulated buyer permanently.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-what-does-ormas-do" question="What does ORMAS do?">
				<p>
					ORMAS is a training architecture that gives your model a complete, causally
					attributed record of every structural event — automatically, during training,
					as a property of how it is built. Not a monitoring wrapper. Not a post-hoc
					analysis tool. The way the model trains. From this capability, we have built
					three solutions: The Proofreader, The Warning Light, and The Diary. Each solves
					a specific business problem.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-download-engine" question="Can I just download and run the engine myself?">
				<p>
					Once the repository is made public (after the review period), yes — the engine
					is MIT-licensed. What you will get: the training architecture, the telemetry
					emitter, the correction mechanism, and the experiment reproduction scripts.
					What you will not get: the artifact generator, the signed diff, the
					cryptographic signing and reader binary, the calibrated bound for your domain,
					or the support and indemnity that make the record usable in a filing. L0 (the
					engine) will be free and openly available. L2 (the deployment licence) is
					where the commercial solution lives. See /licensing for the full stack. The
					repository is not yet public — it is under review.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-proofreader-frozen"
				question="Can The Proofreader attach to any model, or only ORMAS-trained ones?"
			>
				<p>
					This is the open question that gates the solution. In full ORMAS training, the
					per-node readouts are shaped to be informative by design — the local loss
					drives them to encode independent views of the data. That is the property The
					Proofreader uses. A frozen model's layers were not shaped that way. Whether
					they retain enough independence for the readouts to be meaningful is an
					untested empirical question. One GPU-day answers it. We run that measurement
					before any engagement using wrapper mode. If it fails, The Proofreader survives
					as a native-training-mode solution — stronger signal, higher friction for the
					buyer (they need to retrain). Still a solution. Different description.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-warning-light-specificity"
				question="The Warning Light names a failing component. How specific is the attribution?"
			>
				<p>
					We do not yet have a published lead-time number. The paper demonstrates that
					per-node health is a structural property with diagnostic value; the precise
					lead time in steps has not been measured. This measurement is the first thing
					we run before any Warning Light engagement. If the warning is not early, we
					will tell you, and restate the solution description accordingly. We have not
					made a claim we cannot back up.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-data-to-share" question="What data do I have to share to book a session?">
				<p>
					For The Proofreader and The Warning Light: a model checkpoint and your labelled
					data, or access to a training run. We run on-premise if required. No data
					leaves your environment if that is your preference. For The Diary: a training
					session run through the ORMAS architecture (or retrained through it). We
					generate the record from the telemetry. We treat your data under the same
					principles we apply to our own: we do not retain it beyond the engagement, we
					do not use it to train anything, and we do not reference it without your
					explicit permission.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-diagnostic-report" question="What does a diagnostic report look like?">
				<p>
					For The Proofreader: a ranked list of examples sorted by per-node disagreement
					score, split into likely mislabelled / likely hard-but-correct / clean, with a
					breakdown by class and by source if your data carries a source column. For The
					Warning Light: a step-by-step log of health events during the run — which
					component, what diagnosis, what magnitude, at what step. For The Diary: a
					signed, diff-able modification record — node ID, diagnosis, step, magnitude,
					treatment applied, bound respected, correction tensor — formatted for
					regulatory use.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-vs-shap" question="How does this compare to SHAP or LIME?">
				<p>
					SHAP and LIME explain the predictions of a trained, frozen model. They answer:
					"which input features mattered most for this output?" ORMAS instruments the
					training process that produces the model. It answers: "which component is
					structurally compromised, what kind of compromise, and what was done about it —
					at every step." They solve different problems at different points in the
					lifecycle. Use both if your workflow spans both stages.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-vs-wandb" question="How does this compare to Weights & Biases or MLflow?">
				<p>
					W&B and MLflow monitor global training metrics: loss, accuracy, learning rate,
					GPU utilisation. They are excellent experiment trackers. ORMAS monitors
					per-node structural health: which node is dead, which is oscillating, which was
					corrected and why. W&B tells you what happened to your run. ORMAS tells you
					what happened inside your model. Use both.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal" id="the-research">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">THE RESEARCH</Eyebrow>
		<div class="faq-group">
			<Accordion id="q-evidence" question="What is the evidence that this works?">
				<p>
					383 controlled experiments across 4 architecture families. 67 archived runs
					with reproducible seeds. +70.3 pp recovery advantage over standard networks
					under structural collapse. The full evidence base is on /paper. The known
					failure case is also there — we publish both.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-claim-is-transparency"
				question={'The paper says "the claim is transparency; the accuracy gap is its evidence." What does that mean?'}
			>
				<p>
					ORMAS is not a higher-accuracy architecture. In most standard benchmarks, a
					standard network trained on the same data with the same architecture will
					match or exceed it. What ORMAS provides is structural accountability — a
					record of what happened, bounded and causally attributed. The accuracy gap in
					the headline experiments is evidence that the mechanism works, not that the
					architecture should be preferred on accuracy grounds in standard settings. The
					commercial value is the record, not the accuracy delta.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-adversarial-weight"
				question="Adversarial weight injection — ORMAS is 1 pp worse than baseline. Isn't that a problem?"
			>
				<p>
					Yes. It is a known structural blind spot, and it is published rather than
					buried. Adversarially crafted weight perturbations are designed to maintain
					nominal activation statistics while degrading decision boundaries — which
					means they evade exactly what ORMAS monitors. There is currently no mitigation.
					The 1.0 pp deficit is measured and stated. For the markets this architecture
					primarily serves — training monitoring, compliance records, data origin —
					adversarial weight injection is not the primary threat. For defense and
					safety-critical applications, it is, which is part of why that market is named
					but not currently pursued.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-kill-test" question="What is the kill test, and why haven't you re-run it?">
				<p>
					In April 2026, a purpose-built destruction suite was written in advance and run
					against the previous version of the architecture. It failed comprehensively —
					Signal 3 never triggered, ORMAS lost to a plain CNN on every noise level, and
					the ablation arms were indistinguishable from the full system. The current
					architecture has never been re-run through that suite. Re-running it is on the
					immediate task list — it is a regression run, not a new experiment. It is an
					open and named item. It is not hidden.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal" id="licensing">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">LICENSING</Eyebrow>
		<div class="faq-group">
			<Accordion
				id="q-fork-competitor"
				question="The engine is MIT. What prevents a competitor from forking it and building the same solutions?"
			>
				<p>
					Nothing prevents them from forking the engine. This is by design. What they
					cannot fork: the customer's accumulated record, the calibrated bound for a
					specific domain, the artifact format and the L1 licence restrictions that
					protect it commercially, and the fact that somebody signed it and stands behind
					it. The moat is not the code. It is the record, the calibration, and the
					indemnity. See /licensing for the full argument.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-contract-ends"
				question="If my contract with Oxiedo ends, can I still read my compliance records?"
			>
				<p>
					Yes. This is one of three non-standard clauses that must appear in every L2
					contract. On termination: perpetual, irrevocable right to read, retain, and
					reproduce every artifact generated during the term, plus a frozen reader binary
					you keep forever. Your regulatory obligations outlive our commercial
					relationship.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-free-commercial" question="Can I use the engine for free for commercial purposes?">
				<p>
					The engine (L0) is MIT. You can use it commercially, for free, with no
					obligation to us. The artifact layer (L1), the deployment licence (L2), and the
					assurance service (L3) are not free for regulated commercial deployment. If you
					want to use L0 to build something, you are free to do so. If you want the
					record, the indemnity, and the signed artifact, that is L2.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal" id="investing">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">INVESTING</Eyebrow>
		<div class="faq-group">
			<Accordion id="q-raising" question="Is Oxiedo currently raising?">
				<p>
					Yes. We are looking for investors who understand deep-tech primitives,
					long-cycle B2B markets, and the distinction between a validated research result
					and a validated commercial thesis. The research is validated adversarially. The
					commercial thesis has not yet been tested against a paying customer. The raise
					funds the two validation studies, the first three engagements, the legal and
					regulatory consultations, and hiring a technical co-founder. Request the full
					brief at /contact or /invest.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-downside-risk" question="What is the downside risk profile?">
				<p>
					If the validation study underperforms, four planned solutions will be paused.
					Three core solutions remain fully viable: The Proofreader, The Warning Light,
					The Diary. These three require no further research and can be engaged today. A
					company built on three solutions in two critical markets with a licensing moat
					on the compliance record remains a fundamentally sound business. This is the
					stated downside risk. It does not affect the three available solutions.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion
				id="q-strategic-or-financial"
				question="Are you looking for strategic investors or financial investors?"
			>
				<p>
					Both, for different reasons. A strategic investor — a firm or institution that
					operates in one of the five markets — would also be a potential design partner.
					A financial investor needs to be comfortable with: pre-revenue, long-cycle
					markets, one person currently, high evidence-to-customer ratio, the specific
					risk that the validation study fails, and the specific opportunity that it
					passes. If those are the right parameters for your fund, reach out.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section container reveal" id="the-company">
		<Eyebrow as="h2">THE COMPANY</Eyebrow>
		<div class="faq-group">
			<Accordion id="q-why-oxiedo" question="Why is this called Oxiedo? What does it mean?">
				<p>
					It is the company name. The solution primitive is called ORMAS — the public
					solution name and the research codename. The naming question — what brand goes
					on the full solution — is one of the open decisions in the company. A clinical
					brand forecloses four of five markets. A domain-neutral name is the direction.
					The specific name has not been decided.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-one-person" question="One person built all of this?">
				<p>
					Yes. 16,316 lines of code. 85 Python files. 383 controlled experiments. 67
					archived flight recorders. A research manuscript submitted for peer review. A
					30,000-word V2 specification. A 54-file implementation plan. A 35,000-word
					commercial plan. The prior architecture failed its own kill suite in April
					2026. The current one is what came out four months later. That is an unusual
					artifact for an unfunded, one-person effort. It is also incomplete — no
					customers, no second person, no real-world data. Both halves of that statement
					are true and both matter.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-trust-no-customers" question="Why should I trust a company with no paying engagements?">
				<p>
					You should not trust it on faith. We have not asked you to. We have an evidence
					base, a documented mechanism, a commercial structure, and a manuscript under
					peer review. If those are sufficient for your purposes, the solutions are
					available to engage today. If they are not, we would rather you wait than
					engage on insufficient grounds.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-certifications" question="What compliance certifications do you have?">
				<p>
					None yet. SOC 2 Type II and ISO 27001 will be pursued after the first three
					engagements generate sufficient revenue to fund the audits. A DPA template is
					available on /data. We list what we don't have so you don't have to ask.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
			<Accordion id="q-how-to-contact" question="How do I contact you?">
				<p>
					/contact — for solution engagement, research collaboration, co-founder
					interest, and investment inquiries. [REPLACE WITH DOMAIN EMAIL BEFORE LAUNCH] —
					for direct contact. We respond within a few days. Every message is read by a
					person.
				</p>
			</Accordion>
		</div>
	</section>
</Page>
